**Why, in two or three sentences.** Write for somebody who has not seen any of this.
This is a real SQL injection bug. The search field passes user text straight into an SQL query without binding it as a value, so a crafted input can change what the database checks. I confirmed it by sending a malicious search string and seeing the application return all staff records instead of only matching ones.

**What would change my mind.** If new information showed that the input was sanitized or bound as a parameter before running the query, or if the route were rebuilt to reject quotes and SQL syntax, I would reconsider this conclusion.

**How far this answer reaches.** What you established applies to a particular page,
a particular set of data and this version of the application. Say what you have
shown, and be careful not to claim more.
This demonstrates the issue on the staff directory search page in this version of Atrium, using the seeded user records. It shows that a crafted `q` value can alter the SQL on that route, but it does not prove every page or API in the app is vulnerable in the same way.

